38
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:18,030
Okay, so I want to talk quickly about a few different terms that we often hear people mention when we talk about typing and type systems.

39
00:04:18,030 --> 00:04:25,190
I know that typing started in PEP 484 and that was many years ago and that Python has something that's called a type annotation.

40
00:04:25,190 --> 00:04:33,470
Can you tell us a little bit, Danny, what is a type annotation in the Python programming language and how does it fit into the workflow that Sam mentioned a moment ago?

41
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:47,430
So, a type annotation is like some special syntax that you write into your Python program that lets you mark the type of variables or functions, inputs and outputs, attributes of classes, things like that.

42
00:04:47,430 --> 00:04:57,390
So, when you run a Python program normally, the Python interpreter ignores the type annotations and they don't really matter for runtime,

43
00:04:57,390 --> 00:05:02,350
but the type checker will read those annotations and check your code based on that.

44
00:05:02,350 --> 00:05:14,430
So, the element of standardization here is that Python has standardized this way of providing optional type annotations in your code if you choose to do so and type checkers should be able to understand those annotations.

45
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:27,230
Okay, so as a Python programmer, I don't have to put the types in my code, but if I put the types there as annotations or hints, they help Pyre flow to do a better job. Is that the right way to understand it, Danny?

46
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:41,070
Yes, even without annotations, a type checker or language server will try its best to analyze the code and do various levels of type inference to guess the types, but with concrete annotations, typically the type checker performs better.

47
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:42,790
Okay, actually that's really helpful.

48
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:50,790
You mentioned the word inference and often when I learn about type checkers, I remember reading about inference and refinement.

49
00:05:50,790 --> 00:05:52,830
Can you briefly say what those two terms mean, Danny?

50
00:05:52,840 --> 00:06:07,150
So, Sam can correct me if he believes differently, but in my mind, inference is where we are guessing a type when the type is not explicitly specified or annotated.

51
00:06:07,150 --> 00:06:16,110
And refinement is a form of inference because we are guessing a narrower type based on the structure of the code.

52
00:06:16,110 --> 00:06:25,150
So, for example, in a language like Java, you might explicitly cast things, but in Python, it's much more common to assert some like structural properties of the code.

53
00:06:25,150 --> 00:06:33,230
For example, like this variable has you use has attribute, or you check the length of a tuple or something like that.

54
00:06:33,230 --> 00:06:39,110
And these the type checker can understand to get more information about the type, and that's refinement.

55
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:41,790
Sam, do you want to dive in and share any more details?

56
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:45,630
Yeah, I mean, Danny was absolutely right.

57
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:57,590
I think I want to add One more thing on refinement and why it's so interesting in the context of Python is that Python by itself as a language is not statically typed.

58
00:06:57,590 --> 00:07:05,030
So, the way that people write Python code is a little bit different from how you might write Rust or C++.

59
00:07:05,030 --> 00:07:10,710
And as a result, the code that we see and that we have to analyze includes a lot of runtime type tests.

60
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:23,990
So, you might write a function that can be called in a flexible way, maybe a caller can pass a string or a number to your function, and the function will internally check, say, "Hey, is the argument that I received a string?

61
00:07:23,990 --> 00:07:27,550
If it's a string, I'll do one thing, and if it's a number, I'll do something else."

62
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:35,030
And so, it's really important for PyreFly to be able to follow that logic in people's code,

63
00:07:35,030 --> 00:07:46,710
which is why you see this refinement logic in type checkers for dynamic languages like Python and TypeScript, where it's much less common or doesn't exist at all in languages like Rust.

64
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:48,430
Okay, that's helpful.

65
00:07:48,430 --> 00:07:52,910
Now, I've heard both of you mention the word static and then also the word dynamic.

66
00:07:52,910 --> 00:07:59,190
And I know in the context of programming languages or software testing, we often talk about static analysis and dynamic analysis.

67
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:04,950
So, where does type checking fit into static analysis versus dynamic analysis?

68
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:16,510
So, well, type checking can appear in both places, but for PyreFly, it's a static type checker, which means that we don't run any code.

69
00:08:16,510 --> 00:08:22,110
So, we don't get to see what's happening when your program runs.

70
00:08:22,110 --> 00:08:24,030
We are looking at the source code before anything has happened.

71
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:31,870
Okay, so static analysis means you don't run the program, you just study the program's source code. Is that the right way to think about it?

72
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:35,469
Yeah. Okay, awesome.

73
00:08:35,469 --> 00:08:44,070
So, obviously, one of the key goals of a type checker is that we want to be able to find bugs in our programs before we deploy them to production.

74
00:08:44,070 --> 00:08:53,230
And I don't know who would want to jump in here, but could one of you share a concrete example or tell a story of how type checking can actually find a bug in a program?

75
00:08:53,240 --> 00:09:02,190
So, I can give an example of like a everyday thing that you would do in your code base and how type checking can help.

76
00:09:02,190 --> 00:09:06,910
So, for example, if you have a function and you want to refactor it to remove an argument,

77
00:09:06,910 --> 00:09:16,590
the type checker would be able to give you an error at every single call site of that function and say, "Hey, you have to remove the argument at all of those places."

78
00:09:16,590 --> 00:09:25,790
And without it, you would have to grep through the code base and find all the call sites, but you might not find everything because Python is pretty dynamic, right?

79
00:09:25,790 --> 00:09:27,670
You can alias functions, you can pass them around.

80
00:09:27,670 --> 00:09:35,630
It could be a function that has a common name and there's like other functions across the code base that aren't actually the same thing, but have the same name.

81
00:09:35,630 --> 00:09:40,030
So, the type checker just makes that process a lot easier and less error-prone.

82
00:09:40,040 --> 00:09:42,150
Okay, I get it.

83
00:09:42,150 --> 00:09:49,950
Now, one of the things I noticed is that there's many different type checkers or LSP implementations in the broader Python landscape.

84
00:09:49,950 --> 00:10:02,430
So, for example, MyPy is a well-known type checker, and then there are other things that are LSPs like Pyright, and then there's something else called Zubon, which seems to be both a type checker and a language server.

85
00:10:02,430 --> 00:10:12,911
So, I'm wondering if the both of you could work together to give our listeners maybe a broad overview of the Python type checking and LSP ecosystem. Can you help us out?

86
00:10:12,920 --> 00:10:19,710
Yeah, I can give a high-level overview here.

87
00:10:19,710 --> 00:10:29,550
So, you mentioned PEP 484, which is the PEP which introduced type annotations to Python, and this came from MyPy.

88
00:10:29,550 --> 00:10:37,870
MyPy is really the originator of typing in Python. So, MyPy is a great piece of software.

89
00:10:37,870 --> 00:10:40,830
It's been around for a long time. It is just a type checker.

90
00:10:40,839 --> 00:10:43,630
It is not also a language server.

91
00:10:43,630 --> 00:10:50,030
It's written in Python and pioneered a lot of the ideas in this space.

92
00:10:50,030 --> 00:10:58,030
So, later on, I don't know exactly when, but out of Microsoft, there was the Pyright project.

93
00:10:58,030 --> 00:11:01,030
And Pyright is a language server and type checker.

94
00:11:01,030 --> 00:11:09,990
And I think that the reason why Pyright became so popular is that the language server features provide so

95
00:11:09,990 --> 00:11:16,350
much value that it's really I think makes the value of type checking connect with people in a really big way.

96
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:21,590
So, for a long time, the situation was that you had MyPy as the type checker.

97
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:25,070
It had been around for years. People were used to using it.

98
00:11:25,070 --> 00:11:31,829
But then people also started using Pyright in the IDE because the language services are so powerful and useful.

99
00:11:31,829 --> 00:11:40,350
But it's a little bit awkward because then you have one type checker doing analysis for language services, one type checker giving you type errors.

100
00:11:40,350 --> 00:11:44,710
They might not always agree, and it's a little bit of a unfortunate situation.

101
00:11:44,710 --> 00:11:51,030
So, there's this now a new school of type checker that's coming out,

102
00:11:51,030 --> 00:11:57,950
Zovan, Typerfly, that all aim to be both highly efficient and highly compatible.

103
00:11:57,950 --> 00:12:08,790
And so now it's a bit of a I think inflection point in Python typing because there's so many new entries into the space that are all trying to become the all-in-one solution.

104
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:11,190
Okay, that makes sense.

105
00:12:11,190 --> 00:12:19,830
I know many of our listeners may have used a type checker before and they have worked on it in certain cases where it found a bug and

106
00:12:19,830 --> 00:12:24,350
then other cases where it flagged up a problem and then it wasn't really a problem.

107
00:12:24,350 --> 00:12:27,430
So, Danny, this leads me to my next question.

108
00:12:27,430 --> 00:12:35,670
What are false positives and false negatives and true positives and true negatives in the context of a program like Pyreflly?

109
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:45,430
Yeah, so since Pyreflly is a static type checker, it doesn't have access to the information at run time and run time values.

110
00:12:45,430 --> 00:12:53,430
And to a certain extent, it's essentially like reading the code and trying to guess the programmer's intent, especially when there are no annotations.

111
00:12:53,430 --> 00:12:55,550
So, there are cases when you can guess wrong.

112
00:12:55,550 --> 00:13:01,550
So, I would say a true positive is when Pyreflly flagged an error and it was actually a bug.

113
00:13:01,550 --> 00:13:06,270
A true negative is when there's no error and there's no bug.

114
00:13:06,270 --> 00:13:15,910
A false positive is if Pyreflly got confused somehow by the program and flagged an error when there was actually no bug and the code is actually fine.

115
00:13:15,910 --> 00:13:20,870
And then a false negative would be like if there actually is a bug, but somehow it was not caught.

116
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:22,750
Thank you.

117
00:13:22,750 --> 00:13:26,670
So, now my next question is perhaps deceptively simple, but I'm hoping you can tackle it as well.

118
00:13:26,670 --> 00:13:31,310
What does it mean if a program passes a type checker?

119
00:13:31,310 --> 00:13:37,470
So, if I run Pyre fly check in my terminal window and it doesn't give me anything to look at, what do I know about my program?

120
00:13:37,480 --> 00:13:45,390
It means that from Pyre fly's perspective, the program and the type annotations are consistent with each other.

121
00:13:45,400 --> 00:13:47,350
Okay, I get it.

122
00:13:47,350 --> 00:13:56,790
Now, the next thing I wanted to develop, and I'm hoping perhaps Sam, you can help me here, is that I've used multiple type checkers on the same project.

123
00:13:56,790 --> 00:14:02,030
And often times, but not always, they will actually flag up different types of messages.

124
00:14:02,030 --> 00:14:09,950
Can you help our listeners understand why different type checkers might actually flag different concerns about the same Python program?

125
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:11,790
Yeah, that's a great question.

126
00:14:11,790 --> 00:14:18,430
So, there are a lot of different reasons why that might happen,

127
00:14:18,430 --> 00:14:20,830
but I think the biggest difference would be choices that

128
00:14:20,830 --> 00:14:28,150
the type checkers have made about how much to infer,

129
00:14:28,150 --> 00:14:34,990
how much to leave untyped, and then also just how complete of an implementation the type checker has of the typing specification.

130
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:46,590
Okay, so they might disagree, and then I'm going to have to do my diligence in order to discover which one seems to line up best with the PEP specification for typing,

131
00:14:46,590 --> 00:14:52,630
and then maybe look at both of them and then as a developer make an educated decision on what to do next. Is that the kind of workflow?

132
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:56,430
Yeah, and I would say that it's not a great workflow.

133
00:14:56,430 --> 00:15:03,950
So, I don't think it's really a good place to be for the ecosystem to have multiple type checkers that all disagree in significant ways.

134
00:15:03,960 --> 00:15:16,270
Hopefully, if you have an experience like this where you're using two or more type checkers and they don't give you the same exact advice, then at least all the advice you get will be useful.

135
00:15:16,280 --> 00:15:25,390
It's possible that maybe you're using multiple different type checkers and you get some type checkers have false positive,

136
00:15:25,390 --> 00:15:32,671
which is really not useful, or a type checker has a false negative where it's like, "Hey, MyPy caught this, but Pyright didn't. Why not?"

137
00:15:32,680 --> 00:15:34,550
Okay, I see what you're getting at.

138
00:15:34,550 --> 00:15:36,750
So, to summarize really quickly,

139
00:15:36,750 --> 00:15:45,830
I think you're saying that both false positives and false negatives are things that we would like to avoid when we're building a type checker,

140
00:15:45,830 --> 00:15:49,830
and that true positives and true negatives are both good things when we're building a type checker.

141
00:15:49,830 --> 00:15:51,390
Did I catch that the right way?

142
00:15:51,400 --> 00:15:53,910
Yeah, absolutely.

143
00:15:53,910 --> 00:16:01,750
And when we are working on PyreFly, we really look closely at false negatives and false positives.

144
00:16:01,750 --> 00:16:04,190
It's one of the main things that we track from release to release.

145
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:08,670
Did we increase the amount of false positives or false negatives?

146
00:16:08,670 --> 00:16:10,750
If so, we have to follow up and investigate that.

147
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:16,390
So, we look at a corpus of real-world code as we develop to see how we're performing.

148
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:18,030
Okay, that makes sense.

149
00:16:18,030 --> 00:16:22,310
Now, one of the things I wanted to talk about next is the idea of gradual typing.

150
00:16:22,310 --> 00:16:28,230
And additionally, I know that the Python programming language supports something that's called the any type.

151
00:16:28,230 --> 00:16:33,110
Danny, can you help the listeners to understand gradual typing and the use of something called any?

152
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:41,470
Yeah, so I guess the any type is a type that says the value can literally be anything in a very permissive way.

153
00:16:41,470 --> 00:16:46,950
If you have something that's any type, you can pass it into a function regardless of whatever type it takes.

154
00:16:46,950 --> 00:16:56,390
You can do whatever operations you want with it, and it's just signals to the type checker that we have no good information about this type.

155
00:16:56,390 --> 00:17:05,910
So, the type checker will treat it as compatible with anything, and that allows us to not give a lot of false positive errors when we actually don't know anything about the type.

156
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:13,111
Okay. If you use it as an explicit annotation, you can view it as like a backdoor, like, "Oh, this thing is too hard to type. Let's give it an any."

157
00:17:13,111 --> 00:17:20,470
That's one way to use it, but also internally, the type checker will infer any in places where it doesn't know.

158
00:17:20,470 --> 00:17:27,110
Like for example, if you annotate something with just list with no type arguments, then it'll it has to guess a list of any's.

159
00:17:27,120 --> 00:17:28,870
Okay, I see what you're getting at.

160
00:17:28,870 --> 00:17:39,350
Now, I wanted to take a little bit of time to read one quotation, and I saw this on the PyreFly website, and then I'd like to develop the idea of performance after I read the quotation.

161
00:17:39,350 --> 00:17:47,230
So, PyreFly's website says that it can type check over 1.85 million lines of code per second on Meta's infrastructure.

162
00:17:47,230 --> 00:17:49,190
And I have to say, Sam, that's an incredible number.

163
00:17:49,200 --> 00:17:53,670
It's nearly 2 million lines of code per second.

164
00:17:53,670 --> 00:17:57,230
So, can you tell our listeners, why is performance so critical for type checkers?

165
00:17:57,240 --> 00:18:01,510
Well, I would say that performance is critical for all of our developer tools.

166
00:18:01,520 --> 00:18:07,070
I think everyone enjoys when their tools are fast.

167
00:18:07,070 --> 00:18:12,990
And then, not just fast, but there's a moment when a tool responds instantaneously.

168
00:18:12,990 --> 00:18:22,790
And I think at that moment, a tool can really transform, where you don't think about using it, you can integrate it on every keystroke, and it becomes very conversational.

169
00:18:22,790 --> 00:18:25,430
And so, that was one of our goals for PyreFly.

170
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:33,350
That makes a lot of sense. And in fact, you used the word conversational, and it makes me think about using a tool like Cloud Code or Open Code.

171
00:18:33,350 --> 00:18:41,070
I'm guessing that performance is also really critical in the context of agentic software engineering. Sam, can you develop that a little bit further?

172
00:18:41,080 --> 00:18:44,510
Yeah.

173
00:18:44,510 --> 00:18:53,390
So, for the last several months, the industry has changed significantly, and we have been trying to adapt with those changes ourself.

174
00:18:53,390 --> 00:19:02,190
So, it's really interesting, and I think the story is not quite told, but it's true that if an agent can use a type checker like PyreFly as a tool,

175
00:19:02,190 --> 00:19:11,310
and get better information, then it can help the trajectory of that agent interaction be better, tend towards success, because the agent can verify at every step.

176
00:19:11,310 --> 00:19:18,670
And that the more it can verify and the more cheaply it can verify, the fewer tokens you use and the faster your interaction is with that agent.

177
00:19:18,680 --> 00:19:30,390
Okay, so in a way PyreFly can guide the agent towards a correct Python implementation because it's flagging type checking errors, giving that directly back to the agent,

178
00:19:30,390 --> 00:19:40,790
which passes it along to the LLM, and then in a tight loop you can find bugs more rapidly because of the fact PyreFly is so efficient. Did I summarize that workflow in the right way, Danny?

179
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:46,430
Yeah, it's very similar to how a human programmer would use a type checker, too, right?

180
00:19:46,430 --> 00:19:48,831
It tightens the feedback loop. It shifts errors left.

181
00:19:48,831 --> 00:19:59,990
You can see both a human and an AI, in absence of a type checker, maybe they would run the full test suite of the program to verify that their change is correct and sit around for several minutes waiting for the tests to run.

182
00:19:59,990 --> 00:20:07,870
But instead, with the type checker, they would be able to get updated signals on every keystroke or every file save. And that's like a very powerful thing.

183
00:20:07,880 --> 00:20:09,670
Okay, I've got it.

184
00:20:09,670 --> 00:20:13,870
Now, what I want to do next is talk about how you actually built PyreFly.

185
00:20:13,870 --> 00:20:20,630
And I know PyreFly was built in Rust, so I'd like to talk briefly about your decision to use Rust over other programming languages.

186
00:20:20,630 --> 00:20:22,390
Could both of you help our listeners out?

187
00:20:22,390 --> 00:20:24,630
Why did you pick Rust for PyreFly?

188
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:28,230
So, we chose Rust for a few reasons.

189
00:20:28,230 --> 00:20:36,830
I think it helps to get into a little bit of the history of our team because this team had previously built software called Pyre.

190
00:20:36,830 --> 00:20:39,870
So, PyreFly is the successor to Pyre.

191
00:20:39,870 --> 00:20:49,950
And Pyre was written in OCaml, which is a pretty niche functional programming language that's popular with academics and also popular with programming language enthusiasts.

192
00:20:49,950 --> 00:20:52,550
And I love OCaml.

193
00:20:52,550 --> 00:20:54,830
So, I love writing in OCaml and I've done it for the last decade.

194
00:20:54,840 --> 00:21:05,270
But Rust was really a great choice for us because we wanted to build a big open community.

195
00:21:05,270 --> 00:21:10,030
And OCaml has a much smaller developer population, whereas Rust is very popular.

196
00:21:10,030 --> 00:21:14,390
People who don't already know Rust are interested in learning Rust, and a lot of people know Rust.

197
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:24,190
Another big reason for our choice is the just sheer quantity of high-quality third-party code that we can pull in use.

198
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:26,030
Okay, that's really helpful.

199
00:21:26,030 --> 00:21:36,990
Now, since we know a little bit about why you picked Rust, I know that it's connected to Pyre, but you didn't actually reuse any of the code from Pyre inside of your implementation of PyreFly.

200
00:21:36,990 --> 00:21:38,670
Did I remember that correctly?

201
00:21:38,680 --> 00:21:41,150
Yes, PyreFly is a spiritual successor.

202
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:51,390
It doesn't reuse any of the implementation of Pyre, but a lot of the lessons and designs from Pyre sort of influenced how we designed PyreFly.

203
00:21:51,390 --> 00:21:59,830
So, we took some of the things that were good from it, and also things that Pyre couldn't do that we wanted to make sure PyreFly could do,

204
00:21:59,830 --> 00:22:04,350
and try to learn lessons from where Pyre really couldn't scale as well performance-wise and things like that.

205
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:06,550
Okay.

206
00:22:06,550 --> 00:22:17,030
Now, my next question is going to paint with a very broad brush, but with that initial comment out of the way, I'm going to say what I would call the three phases of PyreFly.

207
00:22:17,030 --> 00:22:23,750
So, in phase one, you have to figure out what each module exports or imports, and you have to solve imports transitively.

208
00:22:23,750 --> 00:22:29,910
In phase two, you have to convert modules to bindings, and you have to take into account something called scope.

209
00:22:29,910 --> 00:22:34,190
And then in phase three, you essentially have to solve those bindings.

210
00:22:34,190 --> 00:22:45,950
Now, I recognize it's much more complicated than that, but if I summarize it in terms of those three phases, could the two of you now work together and talk our listeners through those three phases in greater detail?

211
00:22:45,960 --> 00:22:55,470
Yeah, I think that the way you described it is definitely correct, but the first place I would maybe zoom out to is going from one file to many files.

212
00:22:55,470 --> 00:23:00,950
So, if we're just talking about one file, then yeah,

213
00:23:00,950 --> 00:23:10,430
we parse into an AST, we turn the AST into an intermediate representation that we call bindings, which represents the AST after scope resolution has been done.

214
00:23:10,430 --> 00:23:12,550
So, if you use a variable name, where is the definition of that name?

215
00:23:12,560 --> 00:23:18,710
And then we go through this process of solving the bindings.

216
00:23:18,710 --> 00:23:26,990
And the bindings form a graph and the solution step is walking that graph, doing analysis along the way.

217
00:23:26,990 --> 00:23:31,750
The reason it's built that way is for laziness.

218
00:23:31,750 --> 00:23:37,870
So, this is when we zoom out from a single file, we're in maybe a large project with hundreds or thousands of files.

219
00:23:37,880 --> 00:23:46,390
PyreFly is different from other tools in that it's designed as a language service first.

220
00:23:46,390 --> 00:23:55,430
If we were just building a type checker, we'd build it very differently where we would find all the files, we would do for all the files in this batch processing mode.

221
00:23:55,430 --> 00:24:00,550
But when you build an LSP first, you actually have to turn that whole process inside out.

222
00:24:00,550 --> 00:24:05,110
And this is where a lot of the key design decisions come in.

223
00:24:05,110 --> 00:24:07,750
Having this graph that can be lazily elaborated.

224
00:24:07,750 --> 00:24:16,870
So, you start by someone opens a file in the IDE, and you want to be able to give them useful information within milliseconds.

225
00:24:16,870 --> 00:24:21,990
So, you can't even spend time walking the whole file tree to discover what all the files are.

226
00:24:21,990 --> 00:24:26,150
You need to start from an entry point and expand out incrementally.

227
00:24:26,160 --> 00:24:29,270
Thanks, that was both helpful and compelling.

228
00:24:29,270 --> 00:24:33,830
Now, a moment ago I mentioned the idea of solving imports in a transitive fashion.

229
00:24:33,830 --> 00:24:39,110
Danny, could you define the meaning of transitivity in the context of PyreFly and type checking?

230
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:50,030
So, when one file depends on another, it can be direct, which is like, okay, module A imports module B, but it can also be like a transitive thing.

231
00:24:50,030 --> 00:25:00,430
For example, module A imports a class from module B, and that class has a field that's typed using a class defined in a module C.

232
00:25:00,430 --> 00:25:03,790
So, then module A has like a transitive dependency on the module C.

233
00:25:03,800 --> 00:25:05,950
Okay, that's helpful.

234
00:25:05,950 --> 00:25:12,590
And then Sam, I remember a moment ago you were talking about how you turn it inside out when you look at it from an LSP perspective.

235
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:19,150
So, can you tell us maybe from an LSP perspective, what's the scope information that PyreFly uses?

236
00:25:19,150 --> 00:25:23,510
And in particular, what is scope in the context of a programming language?

237
00:25:23,520 --> 00:25:33,030
Yeah, so scope in the context of a programming language is really a simple idea and it's mapping of from names to definitions is I think the easiest way to think about it.

238
00:25:33,030 --> 00:25:45,670
And it's something that developers have in their head at any given time, but when we talk about analysis and the algorithms to perform that analysis, we, you know, we make scope specific concept.

239
00:25:45,670 --> 00:25:53,910
So, if you're in a function and the function takes two parameters X and Y and you add them together, you have this expression in your function X plus Y.

240
00:25:53,910 --> 00:25:56,271
You know, what does X mean? What does Y mean?

241
00:25:56,271 --> 00:26:03,190
The fact that those names map to the parameters, that's what we would include in scope.

242
00:26:03,200 --> 00:26:05,110
Okay, I got it.

243
00:26:05,110 --> 00:26:12,310
Now, I'm wondering if you could tell our listeners maybe a key technical challenge you faced when it came to building PyreFly or

244
00:26:12,310 --> 00:26:18,991
perhaps a story you could tell about improving the performance or the correctness of the tool. Does anything jump to mind?

245
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:26,590
I can think of one and it gets to this core idea of laziness.

246
00:26:26,590 --> 00:26:30,230
So, when you open a file,

247
00:26:30,230 --> 00:26:39,670
we will analyze that file, but we also might need to get information from the files that you import and then transitively from the files that those files import and so on and so on.

248
00:26:39,670 --> 00:26:49,390
So, early in the design of PyreFly, we knew that we had to support this kind of laziness, but we didn't always get it right all the time.

249
00:26:49,390 --> 00:26:56,790
So, one of the bugs that we fixed early on was that whenever you imported a file,

250
00:26:56,790 --> 00:27:03,350
we would eagerly analyze that entire file, which meant that if you had a project with a lot of third-party code,

251
00:27:03,350 --> 00:27:12,270
sometimes you had a project that had maybe two or three files in it, but through its dependencies, the third-party libraries would have millions of lines of code.

252
00:27:12,280 --> 00:27:19,470
And to type-check those files, it took sometimes over a minute when it should have taken just a few milliseconds.

253
00:27:19,470 --> 00:27:26,630
So, we had to increase the laziness in key places so that we didn't end up analyzing all of the third-party dependencies.

254
00:27:26,640 --> 00:27:28,990
Aha, I get it.

255
00:27:28,990 --> 00:27:36,350
So, if I'm a programmer and I'm using Django or I'm using FastAPI, you have to be careful from the perspective of laziness.

256
00:27:36,350 --> 00:27:42,190
If I import FastAPI, maybe you're not going to check everything inside of FastAPI right away.

257
00:27:42,200 --> 00:27:46,390
You just check the parts that it looks like I'm most likely to use.

258
00:27:46,390 --> 00:27:52,030
How do you decide when you have to like overcome your laziness and start to analyze my imports?

259
00:27:52,040 --> 00:27:57,150
So, we have a indexing job that runs when the language server first starts.

260
00:27:57,160 --> 00:28:05,870
So, in addition to this like fully lazy initial initial load that gets you information in the first file that you open,

261
00:28:05,870 --> 00:28:13,310
we also have something running in the background that indexes your project and sort of all the third-party dependencies that it includes.

262
00:28:13,310 --> 00:28:18,910
And that happens in the background and takes, you know, several seconds, but once that's done, then everything like fully works.

263
00:28:18,920 --> 00:28:25,590
But initially, when it first loads, you you already have something working while Pyright is working in the background to solve the rest.

264
00:28:25,600 --> 00:28:27,270
Okay, that makes a lot of sense.

265
00:28:27,270 --> 00:28:35,470
Now, when I was doing some research about type checking for Python, I remember coming across something that was called typeshed.

266
00:28:35,470 --> 00:28:41,950
Does Pyright use typeshed and ultimately, how does this idea of a third-party stub play into the idea of type checking?

267
00:28:41,960 --> 00:28:51,390
Typeshed is a repository of type stubs for various third-party packages that don't have inline type annotations.

268
00:28:51,390 --> 00:29:00,030
This can be for, you know, historical backwards compatibility reasons like, oh, maybe the package needs to work with like older versions of Python that don't have typing,

269
00:29:00,030 --> 00:29:05,710
but sometimes maintainers just don't want the trouble of maintaining maintaining type annotations either.

270
00:29:05,710 --> 00:29:13,470
But for whatever reason, like typeshed is a sort of centralized place that stores these type stubs for third-party packages,

271
00:29:13,470 --> 00:29:20,510
and PyreFly uses them by bundling some of the type stubs to provide, you know, better IDE behavior out of the box.

272
00:29:20,510 --> 00:29:32,630
But if you're writing a Python project, you can also like add these type stub from PyPI as one of your dependencies, and that way no matter which type checker or language server you use, it will know about the types.

273
00:29:32,640 --> 00:29:41,710
Okay, so the way that I'm thinking about is that typeshed is providing type annotations if a project doesn't natively provide the annotations.

274
00:29:41,710 --> 00:29:43,310
Is that the right idea?

275
00:29:43,320 --> 00:29:47,110
Yeah, that's right. Okay, awesome.

276
00:29:47,110 --> 00:29:55,430
So, I know a while ago you mentioned the idea of hovering, and you talked about go to definition, and my understanding is that those are all things that happen inside of the IDE.

277
00:29:55,440 --> 00:30:02,350
So, could one of you tell me like overall, what are the things that would happen in my IDE,

278
00:30:02,350 --> 00:30:11,830
and then what are the types of things that would happen in a CLI, or in an agent coding harness, or in CI/CD? Can you walk us through that whole landscape?

279
00:30:11,840 --> 00:30:19,310
Yeah, so in an IDE, it's very common to, you know, you're reading code, you're writing code, you're navigating.

280
00:30:19,310 --> 00:30:22,710
You might type class and then a dot.

281
00:30:22,720 --> 00:30:28,870
And from the dot, you want to access a method or a field from that class.

282
00:30:28,870 --> 00:30:37,590
So, what PyreFly and other language servers can can do is give you a list of here are all the things that you can access from that class.

283
00:30:37,590 --> 00:30:41,110
So, that's something that you would do while you're writing code.

284
00:30:41,110 --> 00:30:43,990
It's an authoring action, we would say.

285
00:30:43,990 --> 00:30:46,070
Other actions are less about authoring and more about navigation.

286
00:30:46,070 --> 00:30:51,790
So, maybe you say you're reading some code and you see a reference to a class.

287
00:30:51,790 --> 00:30:55,470
You say, "Well, what is this class? What does it do?"

288
00:30:55,480 --> 00:30:57,990
Or function call.

289
00:30:57,990 --> 00:31:07,150
You what you can do is you can command click or control click on that name in the code and PyreFly will navigate will open the file and

290
00:31:07,160 --> 00:31:11,670
navigate to the line where that definition exists in the code.

291
00:31:11,670 --> 00:31:13,310
You can go and read it.

292
00:31:13,310 --> 00:31:17,190
So, those are actions that you would do in navigating or authoring code in the IDE.

293
00:31:17,190 --> 00:31:22,950
In the CLI, the dominant interface is give me the list of all the type errors at this moment.

294
00:31:22,960 --> 00:31:33,430
So, you would run PyreFly check and it would read the code and just give you a list of type errors that you could then, you know, look at and decide which one you want to fix first.

295
00:31:33,440 --> 00:31:35,310
Okay, I see what you're getting at.

296
00:31:35,310 --> 00:31:39,350
Now, just briefly to make sure our listeners are clear.

297
00:31:39,350 --> 00:31:47,270
So, this means that you can use it in VS Code or NeoVim or Zed and then you can also run it in your terminal window or in an agent harness.

298
00:31:47,280 --> 00:31:55,470
I think that you can just do something like pip install PyreFly or UVX PyreFly check. Am I understanding that the right way?

299
00:31:55,480 --> 00:31:59,470
Yes, that's how you would run it on the command line.

300
00:31:59,470 --> 00:32:10,950
In VS Code, you would go to, I guess, the VS Code extension marketplace and just download the PyreFly extension, which automatically launches a PyreFly as a language server when you open a Python file.

301
00:32:10,950 --> 00:32:16,510
And for the unofficial VS Code forks, you would go to the open VSX marketplace, which is similar.

302
00:32:16,520 --> 00:32:18,550
Okay.

303
00:32:18,550 --> 00:32:21,790
Now, many of our listeners may already be using MyPy or Pyright.

304
00:32:21,790 --> 00:32:28,430
Like, they might have Pyright running in their IDE and maybe they're running MyPy on the command line.

305
00:32:28,430 --> 00:32:34,750
So, can you give them some advice if they want to migrate from MyPy or Pyright to start using PyreFly?

306
00:32:34,760 --> 00:32:42,392
What should they do? So, PyreFly actually provides some migration utilities that help when you're trying to switch type checkers.

307
00:32:42,402 --> 00:32:50,350
[snorts] So, I guess at its core it just looks at any existing MyPy or Pyright configuration file for your project and

308
00:32:50,360 --> 00:32:58,550
it tries to generate a Pyreflly configuration file that has roughly the same settings.

309
00:32:58,550 --> 00:33:05,070
So, the error codes are not like one-to-one, but we try our best to like provide mappings between MyPy and Pyright's error codes and ours.

310
00:33:05,070 --> 00:33:12,710
Like different levels of inference, like Pyreflly has a bit more inference than Pyright has, for example.

311
00:33:12,710 --> 00:33:18,110
So, in our migration from Pyright, we actually turn off some of that inference to make it more compatible.

312
00:33:18,110 --> 00:33:21,950
And then if you want to sort of increase the strictness later on, you can.

313
00:33:21,950 --> 00:33:30,470
And another aspect of migration is because these type checkers have sort of different behaviors for the non-standardized parts of the type system,

314
00:33:30,470 --> 00:33:36,910
sometimes it's going to be rare when you're switching from one type checker to another and you get a completely clean check for your first time.

315
00:33:36,910 --> 00:33:43,390
So, another utility that we provide is called Pyreflly suppress, which suppresses all of the existing errors in your code base.

316
00:33:43,390 --> 00:33:45,430
And that makes the initial migration process easier.

317
00:33:45,440 --> 00:33:48,750
And then you would just clean up the suppression comments later on.

318
00:33:48,750 --> 00:33:55,830
Or if you didn't want the suppression comments in your code base, you could set it aside in a baseline file, which is what we provide.

319
00:33:55,830 --> 00:34:01,590
So, it's it's just a text file that on the side that lists all of the type errors that Pyreflly currently emits.

320
00:34:01,590 --> 00:34:06,510
And all of those Pyreflly can read that and suppress them without needing to insert a bunch of comments one by one.

321
00:34:06,520 --> 00:34:08,629
Okay.

322
00:34:08,629 --> 00:34:10,470
Thanks for sharing that about the suppress approach.

323
00:34:10,470 --> 00:34:13,790
I wasn't aware of it, but I can definitely see how it would be useful.

324
00:34:13,790 --> 00:34:23,270
Now, I know many Python programmers actually put lots of configuration for their project inside of the pyproject.toml file. Can you configure Pyreflly using pyproject.toml?

325
00:34:23,280 --> 00:34:26,669
Yes. Okay, that's helpful.

326
00:34:26,669 --> 00:34:32,350
Now, a moment ago and also quite a bit earlier in the show, we talked about the idea of inference.

327
00:34:32,350 --> 00:34:39,070
And I know in addition to PyreFly check and PyreFly suppress, there's also something called PyreFly infer.

328
00:34:39,070 --> 00:34:45,790
Can you develop that a little bit further and then say in a practical way, how does PyreFly infer help me as a Python programmer?

329
00:34:45,800 --> 00:34:55,430
So, PyreFly infer is a tool that fills in a gap that PyreFly check has by design.

330
00:34:55,430 --> 00:35:01,870
So, part of the design of PyreFly is that the inputs to functions are not inferred.

331
00:35:01,870 --> 00:35:10,750
So, if you have a function which takes some number of parameters and those parameters have no type annotation on them,

332
00:35:10,750 --> 00:35:15,430
PyreFly treats them like the any type that we discussed earlier.

333
00:35:15,430 --> 00:35:18,230
So, any use is not checked for errors.

334
00:35:18,230 --> 00:35:25,990
And this is a really important part of PyreFly's design because inferring the type of a parameter is quite difficult.

335
00:35:25,990 --> 00:35:32,910
It requires looking everywhere that that function is called, which could be at anywhere in the program.

336
00:35:32,910 --> 00:35:35,150
And for libraries, it might not even be in your code repository at all.

337
00:35:35,160 --> 00:35:40,390
It could be some dependent, you know, library in another repo.

338
00:35:40,390 --> 00:35:45,190
So, we make the choice, following existing type checkers, to not infer those types.

339
00:35:45,200 --> 00:35:57,390
What PyreFly infer does is a slower kind of one-time or kind of occasionally run analysis that looks at all the uses of functions,

340
00:35:57,390 --> 00:36:04,990
both parameters both within a function and from callers, and says, "You you probably want to have this annotation."

341
00:36:04,990 --> 00:36:07,430
So, it's a way to bootstrap typing for an untyped project.

342
00:36:07,430 --> 00:36:13,230
Where if you have functions with untyped parameters, PyreFly infer will suggest what those probably should be.

343
00:36:13,240 --> 00:36:16,670
Now, I just wanted to connect this to something we talked about before.

344
00:36:16,670 --> 00:36:22,230
We said there's static analysis and dynamic analysis.

345
00:36:22,230 --> 00:36:23,750
Is PyreFly infer static or dynamic analysis?

346
00:36:23,760 --> 00:36:26,310
PyreFly infer is totally static.

347
00:36:26,310 --> 00:36:29,190
There are other tools that you can use.

348
00:36:29,190 --> 00:36:30,830
I'm not as familiar.

349
00:36:30,830 --> 00:36:42,350
I believe one is called MonkeyType, where you can run your program and it will do at runtime tracing of the values that come in through functions. And you can then use that.

350
00:36:42,360 --> 00:36:44,470
Okay.

351
00:36:44,470 --> 00:36:54,750
Yeah, I remember Python's MonkeyTyping tool, and I've actually used it by running my Pytest test suite, and then letting MonkeyType infer through a dynamic analysis what it thinks my types are.

352
00:36:54,750 --> 00:36:59,390
But, if I'm catching you correctly, Pyre infer is a completely static process.

353
00:36:59,400 --> 00:37:02,110
Yeah, that's right. Okay.

354
00:37:02,110 --> 00:37:07,950
Now, I wanted to quickly double-click on issues that are related to performance, because you've mentioned this idea of laziness.

355
00:37:07,950 --> 00:37:18,750
And then also, Danny, you talked about how when the LSP is running, it's like running in the background and doing checks for me, so that when I want to infer something down the road, it can help me out.

356
00:37:18,750 --> 00:37:28,190
So, I know that your documentation says that it's like a lightning-fast approach for auto-complete, or that it has instant feedback, so that it can catch errors.

357
00:37:28,200 --> 00:37:37,430
So, what I'd like to unpack is the secret sauce associated with making it really fast, besides the laziness that we've talked about.

358
00:37:37,430 --> 00:37:40,990
Can you two help us out? What is it that makes PyreFly so fast?

359
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:45,710
I think, you know, one area where we spend a lot of time is parallelism.

360
00:37:45,710 --> 00:37:53,590
So, MyPy is written in Python, and because of Python's global interpreter lock, it is single-threaded.

361
00:37:53,600 --> 00:38:06,710
And Pyright, written in TypeScript, so running on a JavaScript engine, also a single-threaded kind of environment by default.

362
00:38:06,710 --> 00:38:10,190
Rust allows us to write very parallel analysis.

363
00:38:10,190 --> 00:38:15,910
So, we can use all of the CPU cores on your machine to do analysis.

364
00:38:15,910 --> 00:38:22,430
And this is how we achieve this, you know, staggering lines per second metric.

365
00:38:22,430 --> 00:38:28,230
We, in the indexing phase that Danny mentioned are able to chunk up the work and do it in parallel across many cores.

366
00:38:28,240 --> 00:38:30,070
Okay, that makes a lot of sense.

367
00:38:30,070 --> 00:38:34,310
So, parallel processing helps you to be much more faster.

368
00:38:34,310 --> 00:38:40,830
Do you have something in your own development workflow that helps you to like catch performance regressions inside of PyreFly?

369
00:38:40,830 --> 00:38:43,110
And if so, how do you handle that process?

370
00:38:43,120 --> 00:38:51,070
We have some CI jobs that measure the performance along several different metrics.

371
00:38:51,070 --> 00:38:59,150
So, end-to-end like type checking time on a project is one thing, but there's also in the language server for example, when you first open a file, how long does it take to index?

372
00:38:59,150 --> 00:39:02,670
Also, when you make an edit, how fast is the incremental edit time?

373
00:39:02,670 --> 00:39:09,710
So, it's like if you make an edit in one file and you have another file that depends on it, like how quickly do the errors in your second file update?

374
00:39:09,710 --> 00:39:13,830
So, like how long does it take to propagate across the whole project?

375
00:39:13,830 --> 00:39:23,310
And we measure the performance for, you know, various code bases in CI and that's enough to stop, I think, like really catastrophic progressions,

376
00:39:23,310 --> 00:39:32,310
but it does require like right now it requires some vigilance and like profiling and monitoring and running benchmarks before each release to make sure we didn't like, you know, break something horrendously.

377
00:39:32,320 --> 00:39:34,030
Okay, I see what you're getting at.

378
00:39:34,030 --> 00:39:39,350
So, we talked about laziness and I can see how laziness would help in terms of performance.

379
00:39:39,350 --> 00:39:41,190
And then we've also now talked about parallelism.

380
00:39:41,190 --> 00:39:46,350
Is there anything else that either of you would like to say when it comes to what makes PyreFly fast?

