00:39:46,360 --> 00:39:52,030
Danny touched on this and it's a great way to round out the list, incrementality.

382
00:39:52,040 --> 00:40:01,990
So, say that we've started an analysis and we've given you the first round of of type errors for your project and then you make a change.

383
00:40:01,990 --> 00:40:10,670
We don't want to redo all of the work for everything every time you type a single character in your IDE.

384
00:40:10,670 --> 00:40:16,990
So, the other I think major aspect of the design of PyreFly is is incrementally updating the analysis when the code changes.

385
00:40:17,000 --> 00:40:28,030
Okay, so now that we know a little bit about incrementality, is the idea that it's like progressively increasing the scope of the analysis that PyreFly is going to study?

386
00:40:28,030 --> 00:40:29,790
Is that what incrementality does?

387
00:40:29,800 --> 00:40:41,790
So, what I mean by incrementality, the way that it works in practice is that as we do the analysis, and we talked a little bit about chasing dependencies, right?

388
00:40:41,790 --> 00:40:46,030
So, I reference a name that comes from an import that comes from another file.

389
00:40:46,030 --> 00:40:50,350
As we resolve those dependencies, we record that in a dependency graph.

390
00:40:50,360 --> 00:40:57,470
And the dependency graph tracks the dependencies and then also the reverse dependencies.

391
00:40:57,470 --> 00:41:00,350
So, if I have a file, I know all the files that depend on me.

392
00:41:00,360 --> 00:41:10,631
So, when I change, what PyreFly does is it looks at my reverse dependencies and says, "Okay, now you're invalidated, too. And you also need to be rechecked."

