417
00:43:25,000 --> 00:43:30,470
Yeah, so first let me explain what the conformance test suite is.

418
00:43:30,470 --> 00:43:37,190
So, we talked about how type annotations in Python are actually part of the Python language specification.

419
00:43:37,190 --> 00:43:46,350
And this is a really important point and it's what make One thing that makes Python type checking really different from say TypeScript in JavaScript.

420
00:43:46,350 --> 00:43:51,510
TypeScript is an extension of JavaScript, the JavaScript spec, and does not include type annotations at all.

421
00:43:51,510 --> 00:44:03,270
But in Python it's it's a part of the language and there is a council, the Python typing council, whose which is part of the overall Python language governance.

422
00:44:03,270 --> 00:44:10,110
And their responsibility is to specify what these annotations mean.

423
00:44:10,110 --> 00:44:16,230
So, part of their mandate is to maintain this typing specification and

424
00:44:16,230 --> 00:44:26,990
the conformance test suite, which is a automated like CI kind of for do type checkers implement the specification correctly.

425
00:44:26,990 --> 00:44:31,390
So, we have tried very hard and done a lot of work to pass the conformance test suite.

426
00:44:31,390 --> 00:44:41,030
I believe we just recently passed a 90% of tests passing, which is a really a big accomplishment that we're quite proud of.

427
00:44:41,040 --> 00:44:54,990
As it stands today, the conformance test suite covers really a small fraction of what type checkers actually need to do to work in practice on real-world code.

428
00:44:54,990 --> 00:45:02,110
So, when it we decide what we're going to build next, the conformance test suite is of course a factor,

429
00:45:02,110 --> 00:45:12,350
but the much more important factor is, "Hey, let's try this on that corpus of real-world code and see what kind of errors we find or don't find and how do we make PyreFly work correctly?"

430
00:45:12,360 --> 00:45:15,070
Okay, I get it.

431
00:45:15,070 --> 00:45:22,190
So, if I'm understanding this the right way, the higher score that you get on the conformance test suite,

432
00:45:22,190 --> 00:45:28,910
the better job that PyreFly or other type checkers are doing when it comes to fulfilling the PEP and the specification for typing in Python.

433
00:45:28,910 --> 00:45:31,750
Is that the right way for our listeners to think about this?

434
00:45:31,760 --> 00:45:39,870
Yes, although I mean, like I said, the conformance test suite doesn't cover a lot of important things.

435
00:45:39,870 --> 00:45:42,710
So, it doesn't cover anything about type inference.

436
00:45:42,710 --> 00:45:48,590
So, if you write this the very simple program, x = 1, what does that mean?

437
00:45:48,590 --> 00:45:52,910
The specification does not at this moment have anything to say about that.

438
00:45:52,920 --> 00:45:54,710
Okay, I get it.

